  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.4 x² + z²)/2, drawn at half height. It is steeper along x, so overshooting shows there first: zig-zag above learning rate 0.42 and divergence above 2/2.4 ≈ 0.83 (without noise).",
    "Noise is Gaussian, centred on the true slope, with spread 2·sqrt((64−b)/(63b)) for batch size b: largest at 1, exactly zero at 64. Noise comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays the same sequence.",
    "Orange arrow: the actual next step, −learning rate × noisy slope. Grey arrow: −learning rate × true slope. Both drawn at true length in bowl units (scale 1). Hidden, and length shown as 0, once a run ends.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the ball leaves the drawn bowl, |x| or |z| > 2 (diverged). The ball and trail are then held at the bowl's edge; the loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "Running takes 5 steps per second at speed 1. The trail holds the start plus up to 40 step points; the ghost keeps the last run's path.",
    "With noise, a step lowers the loss only on average; single steps with small batches can raise it."
  ]
}